==English==
{{was wotd|2011|July|15}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|volte-face}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/vɒltˈfæs/|/-ˈfɑːs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-I learned some phrases-volte-face.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[reversal]] of [[attitude]], [[policy]], or [[principle]].
#: {{synonyms|en|about-face|U-turn}}
#* {{RQ:Lawrence Psychoanalysis|page=9|passage=Psychoanalysis has sprung many surprises on us, performed more than one '''''volte face''''' before our indignant eyes.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Bigland|title=My Weirdest and Wackiest Rover Yet|journal=Rail|date=2 December 2020|pages=67–68|passage=My next stop is Oxford, which has also grown with the addition of new platforms to accommodate the Chiltern Railways service to London via Bicester&nbsp;– although, short sightedly, the planned electrification from Paddington was canned. Evidence of the '''volte-face''' can be seen along the line at places such as Radley, where mast piles are already sunk or lie discarded at the lineside.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/01/davos-2022-globalisation-wef-joseph-stiglitz|title=Davos 2022 meeting was a missed opportunity over globalisation|author=Joseph Stiglitz|authorlink=Joseph Stiglitz|date=2022-06-01|work=The Guardian|passage=For one-time advocates of unfettered globalisation, this '''volte-face''' has resulted in cognitive dissonance, because the new suite of policy proposals implies that longstanding rules of the international trading system will be bent or broken.}}
# {{lb|en|literature|chiefly|poetry}} A [[dramatic#Adjective|dramatic]] [[change#Noun|change]] in [[mood]] or [[tone#Noun|tone]].
#: {{ux|en|Shakespeare often used '''volte-faces''' in the rhyming couplets at the end of his sonnets.}}

